### 3.2 Key Design Decisions — Backend

#### `main.ts` — The App Bootstrap
```
cookieParser()         → Parses HTTP-only cookies so JWT can be extracted
ValidationPipe         → Globally validates all request bodies via class-validator
enableCors(5173)       → Allows the Vite dev server to talk to this API
```
**Why cookies over `Authorization: Bearer`?** HTTP-only cookies cannot be read by JavaScript, making them immune to XSS token theft. This is the industry-standard approach for web apps.

---

#### `app.module.ts` — The Root Module
This is the composition root. Every feature module must be imported here. It also sets up:
- `ConfigModule.forRoot({ isGlobal: true })` — loads `.env` and makes `ConfigService` available everywhere without re-importing
- `TypeOrmModule.forRootAsync()` — connects to PostgreSQL using `DATABASE_URL` from `.env` and auto-creates tables (`synchronize: true` — only in development)

---

#### Database Entities — Why These 6 Tables?

```
users          → Everyone in the system (admins and voters)
constituencies → Geographic electoral divisions
parties        → Political parties
candidates     → A user registered as a candidate in a constituency for a party
elections      → An election event with a lifecycle (PENDING→RUNNING→COMPLETED)
votes          → A single vote: one voter → one candidate → one election
```

**Relationships:**
```
User ──────────────── Constituency  (voter belongs to one constituency)
User ──────────────── Candidate     (a candidate IS a user, OneToOne)
Party ─────────────── Candidate[]   (a party has many candidates)
Constituency ──────── Candidate[]   (a constituency has many candidates)
Election ──────────── Vote[]        (election has many votes)
Candidate ─────────── Vote[]        (candidate receives many votes)
User (voter) ──────── Vote[]        (voter casts many votes across elections)
```

---

#### Authentication Flow

```
1. POST /auth/register  → Creates user, hashes password with bcrypt, issues JWT cookie
2. POST /auth/login     → Validates CNIC + password, issues JWT cookie
3. POST /auth/logout    → Clears the cookie (expires: new Date(0))
4. GET  /auth/profile   → Returns current user from JWT (guarded)
```

**JWT Strategy** (`jwt.strategy.ts`) reads the token from `req.cookies.Authentication`, decodes it, and fetches the full user from the DB. This means every guarded request has a fresh, database-verified `req.user`.

---

#### Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)

Two roles: `ADMIN` and `VOTER` (stored as a PostgreSQL enum on the `users` table).

```typescript
// How it works:
@UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard, RolesGuard)   // 1. Must be logged in, 2. Must have role
@Roles(UserRole.ADMIN)                  // The required role
@Post()
create(...) { ... }
```

- `JwtAuthGuard` → verifies the cookie token, puts `user` on `req`
- `RolesGuard` → reads `@Roles()` metadata, checks `req.user.role`
- Read endpoints (`GET`) only require `JwtAuthGuard` — both ADMIN and VOTER can view data

---

#### Elections — State Machine

The `ElectionStatus` has a strict transition graph:

```
PENDING ──→ RUNNING ──→ PAUSED ──→ RUNNING
                   ↘             ↘
                    COMPLETED    COMPLETED
```

**Why?** Real elections cannot jump from PENDING to COMPLETED. Enforcing transitions in code prevents data corruption from accidental API calls.

`PATCH /elections/:id/status` with `{ "status": "RUNNING" }` triggers this check.

When status → `RUNNING`, `startTime` is auto-set. When → `COMPLETED`, `endTime` is auto-set.

---

#### Votes — Business Rules Enforced in Code

Three critical rules checked in `VotesService.castVote()`:

| Rule | How enforced |
|---|---|
| Election must be active | Check `election.status === RUNNING` |
| Voter can only vote once per election | `@Unique(['voter','election'])` DB constraint + service-level check |
| Voter must be in same constituency as candidate | Compare `voter.constituency.id === candidate.constituency.id` |

The DB-level `@Unique` is the last line of defense (race condition safety). The service-level check gives a clear, user-friendly error message.

---

#### `GET /elections/:id/results`

Aggregates votes from the database and returns a sorted leaderboard:
```json
{
  "election": { "id": "...", "title": "General 2025", "status": "COMPLETED" },
  "totalVotes": 1024,
  "results": [
    { "candidate": { "user": { "name": "Ali" }, "party": { "name": "PTI" } }, "count": 612 },
    { "candidate": { "user": { "name": "Sara" }, "party": { "name": "PMLN" } }, "count": 412 }
  ]
}
```

